// Title: Potentially Suspicious Command Targeting Teams Sensitive Files
// Sigma ID: d2eb17db-1d39-41dc-b57f-301f6512fa75
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: @SerkinValery
// Date: 2022-09-16 | Modified: 2026-11-21
// ATT&CK: credential-access, T1528
// Description: Detects a commandline containing references to the Microsoft Teams database or cookies files
//   from a process other than Teams. The database might contain authentication tokens and other
//   sensitive information about the logged in accounts.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/microsoft-teams-stores-auth-tokens-as-cleartext-in-windows-linux-macs/
//   - https://web.archive.org/web/20230726144455/https://www.vectra.ai/blogpost/undermining-microsoft-teams-security-by-mining-tokens
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/9493cf5c565d7a01b8f064e01a51e1b94057e4bb/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_teams_suspicious_command_line_cred_access.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (ProcessCommandLine contains "\\Microsoft\\Teams\\Cookies" or ProcessCommandLine contains "\\Microsoft\\Teams\\Local Storage\\leveldb") and (not(FolderPath endswith "\\Microsoft\\Teams\\current\\Teams.exe"))
